WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.330
PRIYANKA VERGADIA: Welcome
to Deconstructing Chatbots.

00:00:02.330 --> 00:00:04.670
I'm Priyanka Vergadia,
and in this episode,

00:00:04.670 --> 00:00:07.250
we will learn how to
create an App Engine front

00:00:07.250 --> 00:00:09.130
end for our Dialogflow agent.

00:00:09.130 --> 00:00:12.028
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:15.892 --> 00:00:17.760
Well, so far in
the series, we've

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:21.090
been focusing on learning the
basics of conversational AI

00:00:21.090 --> 00:00:23.010
and building an agent.

00:00:23.010 --> 00:00:24.930
Once the agent is
built, you may want

00:00:24.930 --> 00:00:27.540
to integrate it with the
front end of your choice

00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:30.370
to give it a look and
feel of your own website.

00:00:30.370 --> 00:00:33.120
Well today, we will learn
to create a custom front

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:35.490
end for our Dialogflow
chat application

00:00:35.490 --> 00:00:38.880
on App Engine using
the Django framework.

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:41.970
You can use any front-end
framework or language you like.

00:00:41.970 --> 00:00:45.460
Django is just an example
we are working with here.

00:00:45.460 --> 00:00:48.810
But before we begin, here
is what we are building.

00:00:48.810 --> 00:00:51.710
There'll be a text input
and a Submit button

00:00:51.710 --> 00:00:54.000
to send chat messages.

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:55.800
All the messages
back and forth will

00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:58.740
be visible on the chat window.

00:00:58.740 --> 00:00:59.240
All right.

00:00:59.240 --> 00:01:01.260
So let's see how this will work.

00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:04.500
The chat app will be
served on App Engine.

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:06.960
We will build our
app in Python using

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:10.650
Django, which is a Python-based
free and open source web

00:01:10.650 --> 00:01:12.360
framework.

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:15.960
App Engine will interact with
Cloud SQL for any database

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:17.700
requests.

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:19.890
And it will interact
with our Dialogflow

00:01:19.890 --> 00:01:23.280
agent via the Dialogflow API.

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:27.240
In this example, we'll be using
the appointment scheduler agent

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:29.850
that we created in
the third episode.

00:01:29.850 --> 00:01:32.280
And we enhanced it
in the eighth episode

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:35.250
to integrate with
Google Calendar.

00:01:35.250 --> 00:01:37.770
Now with that background,
let's dive in and

00:01:37.770 --> 00:01:40.508
see how to make this work.

00:01:40.508 --> 00:01:42.300
Check out the link in
the description below

00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:44.530
to download the
source code for Django

00:01:44.530 --> 00:01:47.760
app we will be
deploying on App Engine.

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:50.070
Let's start by
creating the Cloud SQL

00:01:50.070 --> 00:01:53.160
instance in GCP console.

00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:56.670
Once created, take note
of the connection name.

00:01:56.670 --> 00:02:01.330
Also create the database
with username and password.

00:02:01.330 --> 00:02:04.650
If you don't already
have the Cloud SQL proxy,

00:02:04.650 --> 00:02:06.840
then download and install it.

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:09.720
You'll find the link in
the description below.

00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:12.390
Start the Cloud SQL
proxy with the connection

00:02:12.390 --> 00:02:16.750
name we copied while creating
the Cloud SQL instance.

00:02:16.750 --> 00:02:19.890
This establishes a connection
from your local computer

00:02:19.890 --> 00:02:24.870
to your Cloud SQL instance
for local testing purposes.

00:02:24.870 --> 00:02:28.080
Keep the Cloud SQL proxy
running the entire time

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:31.190
you test your app locally.

00:02:31.190 --> 00:02:34.980
While we are in GCP console,
let's download the service

00:02:34.980 --> 00:02:37.090
account key as well.

00:02:37.090 --> 00:02:42.220
From the hamburger menu,
navigate to the API &amp; Services.

00:02:42.220 --> 00:02:45.670
Click on Create Credentials
button dropdown.

00:02:45.670 --> 00:02:48.310
Then select the
Service Account Key.

00:02:48.310 --> 00:02:51.220
Name the key and make
sure JSON is selected.

00:02:51.220 --> 00:02:53.410
Then click Create.

00:02:53.410 --> 00:02:57.550
The JSON file will then
download on your computer.

00:02:57.550 --> 00:03:01.830
Open settings.py
in MySite folder.

00:03:01.830 --> 00:03:06.250
In two places, replace your
user name and your password

00:03:06.250 --> 00:03:10.000
with the database username
and password you just created.

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:12.730
This helps set up the
connection to the database

00:03:12.730 --> 00:03:17.320
for both App Engine
deployments and local testing.

00:03:17.320 --> 00:03:21.130
Also replace Cloud SQL your
connection name corresponding

00:03:21.130 --> 00:03:23.230
to the host.

00:03:23.230 --> 00:03:26.500
Replace your database with the
name of the Cloud SQL database

00:03:26.500 --> 00:03:28.360
you created.

00:03:28.360 --> 00:03:32.080
When done, close and
save settings.py.

00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:35.230
Now let's set up the Dialogflow
detect intent endpoint

00:03:35.230 --> 00:03:38.470
to be called from
the application.

00:03:38.470 --> 00:03:42.820
For that, navigate to views.py
file in the Chat folder

00:03:42.820 --> 00:03:47.710
and change the Google project
ID to your GCP project ID.

00:03:47.710 --> 00:03:50.380
Appointmentscheduler.json
file is

00:03:50.380 --> 00:03:53.620
used to authenticate and
authorize the app to make calls

00:03:53.620 --> 00:03:55.750
to the Dialogflow API.

00:03:55.750 --> 00:03:59.200
You replace that file with
your own JSON credentials

00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:01.390
that we generated earlier.

00:04:01.390 --> 00:04:04.390
To run the Django app
on your local computer,

00:04:04.390 --> 00:04:08.470
we will need to set up a
Python development environment.

00:04:08.470 --> 00:04:11.450
CD into the project directory.

00:04:11.450 --> 00:04:17.380
Run the command
virtualenv env -p python3

00:04:17.380 --> 00:04:21.029
to create the
virtual environment.

00:04:21.029 --> 00:04:26.030
Now execute source
env/bin/activate

00:04:26.030 --> 00:04:30.110
to activate the virtual
environment we just created.

00:04:30.110 --> 00:04:32.370
You will see env
in the terminal,

00:04:32.370 --> 00:04:36.980
which indicates that you are
in the virtual environment.

00:04:36.980 --> 00:04:42.130
Now execute pip install
-r requirements.txt.

00:04:42.130 --> 00:04:44.080
This will install
the requirements

00:04:44.080 --> 00:04:49.190
for Django, Dialogflow,
and PI MySQL.

00:04:49.190 --> 00:04:52.820
Then run the three migration
commands to set up the models.

00:04:52.820 --> 00:04:57.380
Python3 manage.py
makemigrations,

00:04:57.380 --> 00:05:01.400
makemigration
polls, and migrate.

00:05:01.400 --> 00:05:04.700
At this point, we are ready
to start the local web server.

00:05:04.700 --> 00:05:10.860
For that, execute python3
manage.py runserver.

00:05:10.860 --> 00:05:14.040
Once the server is running,
navigate to localhost

00:05:14.040 --> 00:05:17.210
on 8,000/chat.

00:05:17.210 --> 00:05:20.930
And there, we have our
app running locally.

00:05:20.930 --> 00:05:23.690
Now we are ready to deploy
our app to App Engine.

00:05:23.690 --> 00:05:27.020
For that, we need to
gather all our static files

00:05:27.020 --> 00:05:32.150
in one folder using the
command python3 manage.py

00:05:32.150 --> 00:05:34.100
collectstatic.

00:05:34.100 --> 00:05:38.090
Then run gcloud app deploy
to deploy the application

00:05:38.090 --> 00:05:39.650
on App Engine.

00:05:39.650 --> 00:05:41.960
This may take a few
minutes, but once done, you

00:05:41.960 --> 00:05:44.570
will see the link to
the deployed application

00:05:44.570 --> 00:05:46.160
in your terminal.

00:05:46.160 --> 00:05:50.210
Navigate to the app and verify
that everything's working fine.

00:05:54.700 --> 00:05:57.520
Today, we learned how
to create and deploy

00:05:57.520 --> 00:06:00.010
a Dialogflow front
end on App Engine

00:06:00.010 --> 00:06:02.290
using the Django framework.

